# --- s027 Book I d.4 q.1 = Ord. I d.4 p.1 q.un. (Vat. nn.1-6)
n("n484","s027-01","Circa quartam distinctionem","la","source","= Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 4 p. 1 q. un. (ed. Vat. IV, nn. 1–6), *utrum haec sit vera 'Deus genuit alium Deum'*. In the Vatican edition this part survives only as a short note, sending the reader to the Cambridge question and the Paris *Reportatio* for the arguments (*Argumenta pro et contra quaere Parisius*, n. 2). Gorris's arguments therefore come from the Lombard (*Sent.* I d. 4 c. 1–2) or from the *Reportatio*.","certain")
n("n485","s027-01","nulla prorsus res est que se ipsam gignat vt sit","la","source","Aug. *De Trin.* I 1.1 (*nulla enim omnino res est quae se ipsam gignat ut sit*), quoted by the Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 4 c. 1.","certain")
n("n486","s027-01","audi israel deus deus tuus vnus est","la","source","Deut. 6:4 (Vulg. *Audi Israhel Dominus Deus noster Dominus unus est*). Gorris's form, *deus deus tuus*, reflects a liturgical or patristic paraphrase.","certain")
n("n487","s027-02","cuilibet enti correspondet adequate aliquod ens vel aliquid ens","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 4 n. 3: *Cuicumque entitati correspondens est aliquod ens vel aliquis, sicut suo 'quo'; deitas est de se 'haec'…*. Gorris's *aliquid ens* corresponds to Scotus's *aliquis* (masc.).","certain")
n("n488","s027-02","alia non ydemptitas vt sic","la","textual","A *alia non ydemptitas*, B *alia non identitas*; just before, A *respondet determinate*, B *reputat determinate* (B is probably corrupt for *correspondet*). The sense is that in what is of itself singular (*de se haec*) there falls no further non-identity, i.e. no ‘otherness’ in the same form. Cf. *Ord.* n. 3 (*deitas est de se haec*).","certain")
n("n489","s027-02","brunello","la","commentary","*Brunellus* (Brownie), the stock donkey of scholastic logic, used in examples of non-human individuals (e.g. Peter of Spain, Burley).","certain")
n("n490","s027-03","alius importat duo","la","source","Cf. *Ord.* I d. 4 nn. 4–5 (*'alium' connotat extrema esse eadem in forma determinabili*) and Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 4 c. 2 and d. 9 c. 1. The analysis of *alius* as conveying distinction of supposits plus numbering of the common form is standard (Thomas, *ST* I q. 31 a. 2).","certain")
n("n491","s027-04","genuit non alium deum","la","source","Cf. *Ord.* I d. 4 n. 6 (*Aliud dubium, eundem Deum et alium Deum*). The disjunction *aut alium deum aut alium non deum* is a classic sophism; cf. Lombard, *Sent.* I d. 4 c. 2.","certain")
n("n492","s027-04","fallacia figure dictionis commutando quale quid in hoc aliquid","la","source","Arist. *Soph. El.* 22, 178b37–179a10: the fallacy of figure of speech by changing a *quale quid* (a common nature) into a *hoc aliquid* (a this). Scotus applies it to *Deus genuit Deum* at *Rep.* I d. 4. The *reciprocatio* is the reflexive pronoun *se*.","certain")

# --- B collation (full): B40L–B41L
n("n1111","s027-02","c. Audi israel. deus deus tuus vnus est","la","textual","B: *Audi israel deus*, omitting the rest of the quotation (in s027-01 B also has a single *deus*).","certain")
upd("n492", append=" B: *fallacia dictionis*, omitting *figure*.")
